WEBVTT

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:23.000
The first few months of a baby animal's life are the most crucial.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:30.600
So, if they lose their mothers, they're going to need help.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.040
We are going to be meeting the wild orphans who are getting a second chance.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:42.160
And the dedicated people who devote their lives to saving them.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:49.240
They need the love and the attention because they don't have parents, their parents are taken away from them.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:57.520
In this series, we'll be witnessing the incredible stories of animal orphans from two very different locations.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:05.440
I am in Australia where I'll be meeting Danny, the baby koala in need of round-the-clock care.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.560
There is a real art to it, isn't there?

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.440
And I haven't mastered it.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:16.680
'And Neil, a baby wallaby, 'struggling to stand on his own two feet.'

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.960
It's genuinely exciting, isn't it?

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:27.800
And wildlife cameraman Max Hug Williams steps out from behind the lens to bring us the stories of orphaned wildlife from the rainforests of Costa Rica.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.800
He'll be meeting Newbie the charismatic baby three-toed sloth.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.920
This is definitely the first time I've offered a bunch of flowers to a sloth.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.480
And seeing if she can win her battle with pneumonia.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.880
So, we've just got to all keep our fingers crossed that this new medication works.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:48.640
Saving these wild orphans can be tough.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.560
There'll be ups...

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.400
Good boy!

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.960
..And downs.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:11.919
We'll follow their stories through the most critical, make-or-break months, on their long journeys back to the wild.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:26.000
I've come to Australia, physically isolated from the rest of the world for 35 million years.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:29.760
The animals here are found nowhere else on Earth and the care they need is as unique as they are.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:44.000
I'm on my way south to Cape Otway to meet up with a very special orphan.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:57.560
Here at a local conservation centre, staff and volunteers are dedicated to rescuing koalas and getting these iconic animals back to the wild.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:09.520
They currently have two 19-month-old females, Tilly and Puddles, living in a large outdoor enclosure.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:14.160
Both are thriving and just months away from release.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.960
But I'm here to see the youngest and most vulnerable orphan.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:28.560
Danny, the koala, should be inseparable from his mother.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.000
Instead, he lives inside the centre with carer, Simone.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:38.600
Her mission is to build up Danny's weight and strength, so he can join the other koalas outside.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:45.440
So, we've got him in here in his little makeshift laundry bundle.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:45.880
That's the little man in question.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.000
He's so lush!

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:51.360
Do you want to try and feed him?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.600
Yes, I really do!

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.480
So, if you just support him in the nook of your arm, so he feels safe.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.640
Hello, buddy!

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:09.240
Danny was found on the side of the road, weak and underweight.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:16.840
The man who actually found him saw him on the road and Danny just came running up and crawled up his leg immediately.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.399
So, he was obviously waiting for someone to rescue him!

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:24.480
Although he's known as a koala bear, he's a marsupial and they need very long-term care.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.800
So, Danny is going to be looked after until a year old.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:36.240
Baby marsupials are known as joeys.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:36.920
They spend the first six months or so of their lives developing within their mother's pouch.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:45.320
This is unique to marsupials and gives the mother and infant an incredibly deep bond.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:51.560
'A joey's need for close contact is so strong that Simone has had 'to give Danny a teddy to help avoid stress.'

00:04:51.120 --> 00:05:06.760
They have this instinct to cling on to things and having that surrogate koala mother there means he's more comfortable and is relaxed and we are able to leave him throughout the day with the teddy to hold on to.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:17.960
'In his mother's pouch, Danny would have continuous access to her milk, 'so Simone feeds him every two hours, around the clock.'

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.160
What do I need to do?

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.960
So, if you just hold the teat and offer it to him, leaning his head back.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.360
So, leaning his head back...

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:25.280
Yeah.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:25.840
..And then just offer a little bit of this. Yeah.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.920
Not in the nose, Danny, sorry!

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:31.480
Just aim for the teat in the mouth.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:39.640
'This marsupial milk substitute is specially formulated to be 'high in fat but lactose-free.'

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.680
Oh, squeeze, OK.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.640
That's perfect.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.520
'Just like the real thing.'

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.360
Ooh!

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:50.400
Oh, yeah, that all right, we can just give him a clean.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.880
He's a very messy eater, so we're used to washing him.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:57.080
You're pretty used to that?

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:01.040
'But feeding Danny is not straightforward.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:09.920
'Marsupial mouths are very delicate, so the teats on the bottles 'have to be made of very soft rubber and the milk has to be lapped.'

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.160
That's all good!

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:15.760
There is a real art to it and I haven't mastered it!

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:20.480
Not the most successful feeding session but that's partly because Danny is nocturnal.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:31.240
Koalas spend up to 20 hours a day snoozing and digesting their food and are most active in the middle of the night.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:41.600
'I've stayed over to see if Danny is a different character 'when the lights go out.'

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.120
He's coming outside.

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:50.320
When he's awake he makes shuffling noises in the basket.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:55.640
He's planned his grand escape!

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.520
Hey, little man, let go, let go!

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.000
Oh, he's very wakeful, isn't he?

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:10.360
'If Danny's going to be strong enough to join the bigger females outside, 'he has to build enough strength in his muscles to climb trees safely.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:17.960
'Simone's solution... energetic nightly games of chase with his teddy.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:23.360
'In the wild, his mother would tip him out of the pouch...

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.760
Danny, Danny, oi, oi, oi...

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:30.400
'..And encourage him to climb and explore the trees.'

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.640
Do you want a game!

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:35.760
'Exercise over, he finally gets his prize possession back.'

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.360
And he relaxes pretty quickly.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.880
'Now he's nice and relaxed, it's time for another feed.'

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:48.720
Look how alert he is!

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:58.160
'Simone has been doing this regime of feeding and exercise '24 hours a day for the last six weeks!'

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.440
I bet you feel more forgiving in the morning, don't you?

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.240
In the night-time it's no mercy!

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.840
Frustration levels get very high during the night.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.920
Then you wake up and see him in the morning and see his face and it's all forgiven!

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:22.880
'If Simone can keep up this demanding schedule, 'Danny should be on target for the next big step on his journey 'back to the wild, joining the other two koalas outside.'

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:27.440
It will be a happy day when he goes out into the enclosure then, won't it?

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:37.520
Yeah, I mean obviously it's a huge moment in his development but also it's a huge moment for us getting our nights back.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:42.640
'But moving outside will be tough.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:47.760
'No more night feeds, no more teddy and no more Simone.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:53.360
'He needs to be resilient enough, both physically and mentally, 'to cope.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:57.200
It's going to be a huge challenge.'

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:04.400
This same resilience is needed by orphaned animals on the other side of the world.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:10.720
Costa Rica forms part of the land bridge connecting North and South America.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:19.240
It's a biodiversity hot spot with lush jungles and abundant wildlife.

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:24.760
But despite strict protection, Costa Rica still has its fair share of orphaned wildlife.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:30.760
Max Hug Williams is following their stories.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:37.400
Costa Rica is a haven for wildlife with some of the most incredible species on the planet.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.880
But, like anywhere where animals are living so close to humans, there are always going to be problems.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:48.320
'Roads bisect its vast forests...but wildlife 'and cars are not good neighbours.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:54.440
'As a wildlife cameraman I've occasionally witnessed 'the heartbreak of youngsters losing their mothers.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:01.480
'I want to find out what happens to the lucky few who get rescued.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:09.760
'Here on Costa Rica's Pacific coast, 'KSTR provides a sanctuary for orphaned wildlife.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:16.360
'Wildlife manager Sam's main responsibility is 'caring for the centre's numerous baby sloths.'

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.640
Brushing my teeth, I have sloths on me, washing my face, I have sloths on me.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.400
I have literally changed clothes with sloths on me.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:29.040
You name it, I've probably done it with a sloth on me.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:36.600
'Sam's small apartment has been taken over by these enigmatic 'characters who demand round-the-clock care.'

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:44.400
Just dropping in on Sam's place to see what life's like living with six sloths.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:44.680
Hey, Sam.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.960
Hi.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:48.440
It's complete carnage in here!

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.600
It's like the morning after a big sloth party.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:58.080
It's a disaster...and I wish I could say that this was rare, but this is pretty much a daily occurrence.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.880
They climb the curtains, they climb the chairs.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:06.240
They love their climbing apparatus but there's nothing that will stop them from exploring.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.680
So, it even went a little too far last night.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.520
Do you want to see where they are?

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.240
What's happened?

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.600
What?

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.440
They're asleep in the bottom of your La-Z-Boy.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.080
Yeah!

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:23.280
It's ridiculous!

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:31.800
'This is Pelota and Kermie, the nocturnal two-toed sloths.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.040
'Both lost their mothers and are living with Sam 'till they are old enough to go back to the wild.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:43.360
'Every night they spend their time foraging for food, 'and wrecking Sam's flat, until the day shift takes over.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:52.000
'That's when Minnie and Newbie the three-toed sloths wake up, 'and need Sam's undivided attention.'

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:52.480
I can't get them out.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.880
You can't get them out?

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:57.040
No, there's literally no way to pull them out, they have to come out on their own.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.920
You've got to make sure you don't give her any more food in there, cos she's pretty big already!

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:00.920
I know!

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.120
If she ate a lot in there, she definitely wouldn't come out.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:09.480
I think the only reason she fit in is cos she recently went to the loo.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:19.480
'But of all her sloths, five-month-old Newbie 'is the one causing the most worry for Sam.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:24.800
'Newbie has been battling with pneumonia since she was rescued.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:31.600
'Sam thinks it was triggered by the stress of losing her mother.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:43.600
'Not wanting to tempt fate by giving her a proper name, 'Sam simply calls her Newbie 'and the two have formed a very strong bond.'

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:50.040
Any baby needs someone to take care of them when they're younger.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.200
Certain species need more than others.

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:59.320
Humans need a lot of maternal investment and sloths are similar.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:05.680
So, for an orphan that we receive, they need a surrogate mother.

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:10.080
For Newbie, at the moment, she is with me pretty much all the time.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:24.520
In the wild Newbie would have clung on to her mother until about nine months old, feeding on her milk and learning what to eat.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.520
Now that job falls to Sam.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:29.720
I'm going to give her some hibiscus flowers now.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:34.160
This is one her favourite natural snacks, I usually get her to drink her milk first.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:39.800
It's kind of like eating your vegetables before you get desert.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:43.440
She loves them.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:51.960
Like many herbivores, sloths have enzymes in their saliva that help break down plant matter.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:57.400
They basically dissolve in her mouth like chocolate.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:03.880
Even for the experts like Sam, raising sloths is notoriously difficult.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:17.480
There is a very good chance that she won't make it and that's why we're doing everything we can to increase her chances of surviving to adulthood and one day going back to the wild.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:30.080
'Sam's fears come from painful experience, 'and in Newbie's case, several courses of antibiotics 'have so far failed to cure the infection.'

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.520
Nothing here is simple.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:45.560
When an animal comes in completely vulnerable, only a few days old, and often with some disease or illness, it's literally a battle to fight to keep them alive.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:56.760
And it's only once you get them through those hard first few weeks that you can even start the long, long journey of teaching them how to be wild again and giving them any hope of getting back out in these forests.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:08.000
'Despite her ongoing treatment, Sam thinks Newbie might just be 'well enough to begin learning some basic skills.

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:14.680
'She's building her a baby climbing frame.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:30.560
Even though she's not 100% out of the woods, it's time for me and for her to start thinking about getting ready to be in the wild, so she needs to start practising climbing.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:39.480
For Newbie this is a new and vital test.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:46.600
Wow, she didn't waste any time!

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:49.840
She likes it!

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.160
She actually likes it.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:16:03.120
I mean she's a sloth, so it's not like she's going to zoom from one side to the other really quickly but she's definitely on it, she doesn't want to get off.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:19.200
People think that sloths are just really slow and really lazy and sleep all the time but what they don't realise is their body isn't made up of the type of muscle structure that would allow them to move quickly.

00:16:18.320 --> 00:16:25.800
That's not what they need to do, they're more about moving deliberately.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:33.280
I can't help but be a little proud of her, she's been through so much and she's still a little bit sick but she's not giving up.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:47.440
She's very interested in learning, she loves exploring and I think that those characteristics show that she wants to live, that she wants to go back to the wild.

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:55.400
It's real progress and the next step will be to see if she can cope with a real tree.

00:16:54.440 --> 00:17:08.480
If Newbie makes it to release as an adult wild sloth she will have to contend with all the dangers that got her here in the first place.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:20.520
Like so many sloths in Costa Rica, Newbie's mother came to the ground to cross a road and that's when Newbie was orphaned.

00:17:20.319 --> 00:17:34.640
Roads and human developments are a major issue for wildlife the world over and 9,000 miles away, Ellie is about to meet another tiny casualty.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:56.520
In Australia, tens of thousands of animals are killed on the road every single year but astonishingly, because they are protected by their mother's pouch, many of the joeys still survive.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:18:09.160
25 years ago, Stella Reid gave up her career as an air stewardess to rescue some of these vulnerable joeys.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:15.280
Right now she has 20 living with her.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:25.960
Sharing my life with animals and living so close to them, for me, is probably the closest thing to being in heaven.

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:35.240
Most of Stella's orphans are kangaroos and wallabies and belong to the family known as macropodiae, which is Latin for "long foot".

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:42.840
The main thing that separates the two is size - wallabies tend to be smaller.

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:45.360
But what unites them is that as orphans they need specialist care.

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:55.320
'Stella has devoted half her life to perfecting 'the techniques for raising them.'

00:18:54.680 --> 00:19:03.280
Stella is so dedicated to rearing kangaroos and wallabies that's she had her whole housed adapted so she can do it.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:17.480
This is actually her bedroom, as you can see, and it's called "the baby nursery" because the teeny tiny ones will go in there, so that if they get distressed in the night and need to hear a heartbeat she can pick them up and put them on her chest.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:26.520
If we carry on out here, this floor is made out of recycled tyres, so that it doesn't hurt their feet when they bounce and they don't skid like they would on tiles.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.880
Hello, Stella.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:32.600
Just over there we've got a wallaby on a throne over there.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:39.600
These windows have been placed deliberately high up so that if they hop around they don't potentially jump through them and injure themselves.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:42.200
This is Harry, hello, mate.

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:48.320
He's a kangaroo and not a wallaby, making himself at home.

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:54.160
So, through to the main part of the house, and the last room is the outside nursery.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:58.160
If we come on in, it's a bit like a sixth form common room.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:06.240
They're all slightly older and in their pouches, just hanging out, making the most of things, all these different pouches.

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.480
Let's see if Harry will go into a pouch!

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:10.240
Harry, are you going to go into a nice snugly pouch?

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:10.600
Here you go, mate, do you want to go in there?

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:11.320
Oh, look at this...he's a big boy!

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:15.280
This instinct for going into the pouch is so strong, he just climbs straight in there...tumbling around.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.880
I'll show you how it works...

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:26.320
They go into those pouch and then into one of these pouches on the wall just like this.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:30.360
There you go, Harry, look.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.840
There he is, snug!

00:20:31.160 --> 00:20:41.360
The amazing thing about this room is there's also this specially designed "roo door", so that they can get in and out if they want to, any time.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:43.160
Ooh, I can barely squeeze through!

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:43.320
Slightly undignified...

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:44.920
I'm out!

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:50.080
So, they've got pretty much everything they need right here.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:21:10.000
But although Stella is inundated with roos and wallabies, none of them take up as much of her time as young wallaby, Neil.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:28.800
He's an orphan from a car accident where his mum was killed and Neil's been with us one week.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:32.600
Neil is just 1 months old.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:38.280
In the wild he would be around nine months before he left his mother's pouch.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:45.400
He doesn't have a second coat, he's just got the light top coat that he has while he's in his mum's pouch.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:48.880
We do call them little velvets.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:22:04.160
I'm covering his eyes a little bit, in Mum's pouch, it's darker than here.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:08.160
He's getting a bit used to it, so it's not too stressful.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:14.440
In the first three days here, he cried out at night-time for his mum.

00:22:13.480 --> 00:22:16.320
It's heartbreaking to listen to him call out for his mum.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:25.440
What we do then is pick him up and put him in the bed with me so he can felt my heartbeat.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:35.640
The moment I picked him up and cuddled him... ..and held him next to my heart the crying stopped.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:48.080
Because they develop for so long in her pouch, the beating of their mother's heart is deeply comforting for baby marsupials.

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:58.920
Research shows that maternal nurture physically changes brain chemistry and alters the way a youngster's body responds to stress.

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:08.880
Without this closeness and reassurance orphans have been known to die of anxiety.

00:23:08.040 --> 00:23:19.160
But with new animals arriving all the time, Stella can't always be there for Neil, and he urgently needs a companion the same size and age who he can hang out with.

00:23:29.320 --> 00:23:41.840
But for now, Stella is filling the role of both companion and foster mother and she thinks he might be ready to hit the biggest developmental milestone in his life so far.

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:49.960
Leaving the pouch and standing on his own two feet for the first time ever.

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:51.440
It is a copy of what Mum does in the wild.

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:59.120
They tip the baby out for a split second and then they let them hop back in again.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:06.200
Get your balance...there you go.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:19.720
So, he's using his little hands for balance.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:44.320
Once fully grown, Neil will be able to hop at a top speed of 30mph.

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.760
For now, just staying upright is tricky.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:56.520
Oh, wow, it's beautiful.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:59.320
He did really, really well!

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:06.520
I think his little mind would have been saying, "What's going on?"

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:14.840
And the next time when I let him out it'll be a few seconds longer and the time after that, a few seconds longer.

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:20.520
Within a week, he'll be out of the pouch for about 30 seconds, at the most.

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:30.840
He might have taken his first few steps but he's still very weak and vulnerable.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:33.360
What's Mummy doing?

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:38.000
The need to find him a companion is growing increasingly urgent.

00:25:37.040 --> 00:25:45.480
He's too small and fragile to join the older wallabies, but he simply won't develop and thrive if he stays on his own.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:52.520
Stella's long-term goal is for him to establish a territory among the wild population of wallabies.

00:25:53.920 --> 00:26:05.200
Over the years, she's helped return over 1,000 orphans back to the wild.

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:08.720
But Neil's release is a long way off.

00:26:07.840 --> 00:26:18.240
Like all our orphans, the journey back to the wild has to be taken one step at a time.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:32.600
Staying in Australia, Danny the koala is about to take another step on his journey, graduating to the outdoor enclosure.

00:26:32.200 --> 00:26:43.120
Until now he's spent his time indoors, being fed, exercised and pampered.

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:49.560
Lizzie, the rescue centre manager is on hand to oversee events.

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:54.800
She needs to be sure Danny is ready for this big step towards independence.

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:57.280
And if he weighs in at a kilo or more, then he will get the green light.

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:04.440
Simone is hoping all her hard work has paid off!

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.960
He's over a kilo!

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:14.720
Good job, little man, well done, that's fabulous!

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:28.200
The plan is to take Danny outside for short training sessions, gradually building his confidence over a few weeks until he is able to let go of his teddy and move out completely.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:31.800
It's pretty nerve-racking bringing him out here for the first time.

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:38.720
Koalas do have a very innate sense of what they should be doing but you still always worry.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:39.040
There you go!

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:40.400
Good boy, that's it!

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:43.480
What a brave baby!

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:55.800
This is the first tree Danny has ever encountered!

00:27:56.560 --> 00:28:03.840
He was so young when he was rescued, he had only ever been in his mum's pouch.

00:28:02.880 --> 00:28:05.520
Good boy!

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:09.800
He's not sure what to do!

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:16.360
Koalas have sharp claws designed for gripping bark.

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:20.400
Do you want to climb up to the leaves?

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:20.760
Come on!

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:24.520
Which Danny is putting to the test.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:33.840
Good muscles, Danny!

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:36.680
OK, getting a bit upset now.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:40.200
You just need a warm pouch.

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:46.080
It's a great first step but he's got a lot more work to do.

00:28:45.120 --> 00:28:47.360
Well done!

00:28:48.760 --> 00:29:05.680
Over the next few weeks, Danny is put on a boot camp regime of feeding and climbing on branches in preparation for his future life outdoors.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:28.480
Slowly his confidence begins to build.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:28.720
Hey, Danny!

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:43.280
But learning to climb trees requires complete concentration.

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:43.600
Oh, Danny!

00:29:43.400 --> 00:29:44.760
He's OK, he's OK, he's learning.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:47.120
He has to learn!

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:54.520
Luckily, Danny is superbly adapted to a life in the trees.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:55.320
We're going up!

00:29:54.440 --> 00:30:06.640
Apart from those sharp claws, he also has a bony plate in his lower back which acts like a built in chair for resting in the fork of trees.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:11.320
By week three, he's started to look and behave like a wild koala.

00:30:12.760 --> 00:30:16.640
Danny has advanced a lot since the last time we took him outside.

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:25.520
He's climbing and eating and generally more comfortable in the enclosure.

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:32.480
After a month in training the team decide he's ready to make the move outside full-time.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:37.640
So tonight will be his last night indoors with Simone.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:42.640
Tomorrow he'll be introduced to the two older female koalas.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:47.600
But letting go of the security of his teddy and the closeness of Simone will be tough.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:31:01.920
Everyone is hoping that one of the two older koalas will take young Danny into her care.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:08.600
On the other side of the world in Costa Rica, another little orphan has a lot to cope with.

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:17.440
Newbie, the three-toed sloth, has been battling with pneumonia for over eight weeks.

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:30.720
But every day she is getting stronger, and Sam is hopeful that she may be beating this bacterial infection in her lungs at last, but Sam isn't taking any chances.

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:42.480
Every morning and evening, Newbie is given a cocktail of medicines, delivered to her in a mist with pure oxygen.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:45.000
She tolerates it really well.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:52.160
Part of me wonders whether it feels good, especially the bronchodilator because it helps her to breathe.

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:53.840
But Sam believes that there's more to treating Newbie than just medicine.

00:31:53.840 --> 00:32:00.240
As anthropomorphic as it sounds, they really need what we call love.

00:31:59.440 --> 00:32:19.280
With baby animals, and especially sloths, they need a lot of attention, a lot of reassurance, they need to feel safe, I think that because stress has such an effect on their body, without that comfort and safety they just don't thrive and their body shuts down.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:32.640
Sam believes that without this close care, no amount of antibiotic would save her.

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:34.840
I'm hopeful.

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:50.480
She's responding well to treatment, it's just a case of nipping this pneumonia in the bud so that way we don't have to worry it will come creeping up and silently kill her one day.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:53.880
I just don't want to name her yet.

00:32:53.160 --> 00:33:03.720
Once she gets through this and we're confident she's doing well then we'll reassess the naming situation, and hopefully pick something more dignified than "Newbie".

00:33:06.600 --> 00:33:16.760
If Newbie is ever to go back to the wild, her preparation for release has to continue.

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:26.280
'So Sam is keen to keep building her confidence in climbing, 'and thinks she's ready for a real tree.'

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:31.040
Newbie, first tree!

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:35.800
I'm just going to try and get her close to the tree.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:41.600
This is basically the beginning of her getting back to the wild something more natural.

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:42.480
Being a sloth again.

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:42.840
Exactly.

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:47.360
So this is very typical - she has one hand on the new spot and one hand on the mom.

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:50.080
She's not going to leave me totally at first.

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:53.440
'Like Danny the koala, Newbie is a little hesitant at first 'but soon she's off at her own pace.'

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:06.520
It certainly looks like she's ready to explore.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:13.360
You've got to be patient, lots of baby steps, lots of milestones to reach.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:13.960
You definitely have to be patient with sloths.

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:22.719
Everything happens at a little bit slower speed.

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:28.719
'Despite their reputation for laziness, 'sloths only sleep for around nine hours a day.

00:34:27.920 --> 00:34:41.400
'Their slow movements are a way of conserving energy, 'a neat survival strategy, allowing them to eat 'nutrient-poor leaves that won't sustain other animals.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:45.520
'Sloths have the slowest digestion of any mammal.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:50.000
It can take them 'two weeks to process a single meal.

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:56.239
'Their entire lifestyle is all about expending as little energy as possible.'

00:34:55.360 --> 00:35:00.320
Look at that, she's climbing, feeding...

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:01.240
You're obviously a pretty good sloth mum.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:03.240
I think she's doing really well, I'm very proud of her.

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:15.360
'This has been a big step for Newbie, but at just five months old, 'it's not long before she needs the reassurance of her surrogate mother.'

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:19.200
I think she might be coming to me now.

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:19.600
On the move, had enough.

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:20.360
Mm-hm.

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:24.360
That was a pretty good effort for day one.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:28.720
They're not adapted to moving on the ground, are they?

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:32.040
A sitting duck for any predator.

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:32.480
I know, that's why it's so sad.

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:39.560
That's why her mom was attacked by a dog, because as soon as they come to the ground she has no defences against a dog.

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:52.040
'Sloths have a top speed of just 30cm-per-minute, 'which means that when they do come to the ground, 'they are completely vulnerable.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:36:03.040
'For Newbie, Sam represents safety and security, as well as her only 'chance of beating her illness and getting back to the wild.

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:07.360
'There is still a long road ahead for both of them.

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:10.600
'And one that will inevitably be travelled at a very leisurely pace.'

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:26.520
It's a different story here in Australia.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:32.800
Macropods, like kangaroos, can cover seven metres in a single hop.

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:39.720
'But every journey starts with the first steps.

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:47.560
'And for Neil, the orphaned wallaby, they are very tentative ones.'

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:49.320
A little bundle in there!

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:50.520
Oh, wow!

00:36:49.840 --> 00:37:02.800
'With Stella's careful attention to his diet, 'he is growing and gaining in strength, and she is getting to know 'the intimate workings of his digestive system.'

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:09.880
Little Neil was under 800g when he came in.

00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:23.960
He's on three bottles a day at the moment, he goes to the toilet very well, he's got solid droppings, but they fart, and they can clear a room, and quite often people will all look at each other, it is so bad.

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:24.960
Accusingly!

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:26.160
Accusingly.

00:37:25.400 --> 00:37:29.120
It can't be the sweet one!

00:37:28.440 --> 00:37:32.600
He's actually the sweet one.

00:37:31.960 --> 00:37:33.080
He's actually a wallaby.

00:37:32.320 --> 00:37:38.440
His true name is black wallaby or swamp wallaby.

00:37:37.600 --> 00:37:49.760
But his original name was stinky wallaby, and the aborigines rarely ate them because they smelt, they farted, which saved their lives.

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:53.480
That's a really good...

00:37:52.520 --> 00:37:57.200
If you fart, it can save your life - it's a good evolutionary mechanism.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:03.800
'But if Neil is to survive, he needs to learn to hop.'

00:38:04.240 --> 00:38:08.880
Put your hands under his chest here, that's it.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:10.320
A balance rather than a lift.

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:11.080
Hold just his tail...that's it.

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:16.560
Oh, so wobbly!

00:38:23.400 --> 00:38:30.640
Just bring his pouch back in front.

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:31.080
Get it ready, just in case.

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:37.680
You hold his hands now and you can just balance him there, and let him get his balance.

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:38.680
Beautiful.

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:43.840
It's genuinely exciting, that sense that he's taken these initial steps, it's so meaningful.

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:44.120
Wow, there's a hop!

00:38:43.160 --> 00:38:45.720
That's fantastic!

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:47.200
So sweet.

00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:50.000
Back to the pouch.

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:50.800
That's it.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:51.360
What an adventure!

00:38:52.520 --> 00:38:55.840
Now you're a wallaby mum.

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:58.720
I feel proud, proud of those steps.

00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:09.560
'But there is something even more important for Neil than 'developing his walking skills - he needs a companion.'

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:27.760
In the wallaby world, his mum's with him 24 hours a day, seven days a week, but I have to do other things, I have to look after other animals and go outside, and although I do carry him with me, he can't be with me 24 hours, whereas a little friend can be.

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:28.720
So we need to find a little friend for Neil, then.

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:31.160
Yes, we do.

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:38.440
But they come in all the time, because the cars are getting faster and the little creatures are crossing the roads.

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:42.680
So we're getting more and more than we've ever had before.

00:39:49.960 --> 00:40:04.120
For sensitive baby marsupials like Neil, finding a suitable companion could make the difference between life or death.

00:40:04.920 --> 00:40:07.000
Fortunately, Stella has a plan.

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:18.840
Until an orphan the same age and size is brought to Stella, she's going to see if Neil is strong enough to try hanging out with a small band of older wallabies nicknamed the Three Amigos.

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:25.720
Like Neil, all three of them lost their mothers in car accidents.

00:40:26.200 --> 00:40:36.320
Mitchell is quite small but he likes to bounce around, and he jumps on everybody and makes everybody hide in their pouch.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:44.520
Max just likes to be adventurous and he hides under the furniture.

00:40:43.720 --> 00:40:46.720
Three great little characters.

00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:54.400
The only problem is that they are a whole month older than Neil.

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:20.200
But they are just that little bit bigger and bouncier than Neil.

00:41:19.240 --> 00:41:27.640
He simply isn't mobile enough to join the fun, and could accidentally get hurt.

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:32.160
Maybe the pouch is the safest place after all.

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:40.880
For Neil and Stella, it's going to be a waiting game, till the right wallaby comes along.

00:41:56.160 --> 00:42:01.320
Here in Costa Rica, there's an altogether different waiting game going on.

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:10.200
Newbie, the orphaned three-toed sloth, is under close observation.

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:25.120
Sloths only go to the toilet once or twice a week, so sloth carer, Sam, has to try and predict it and get her to the right place at the right time.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:37.640
Sloths can lose around 30% of their body weight in one poop and pee, so that's a lot, I wish humans could do that sometimes.

00:42:36.760 --> 00:42:45.920
Wild sloths spend all of their time in the tops of trees, only coming to the ground every few days when nature calls.

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:53.160
And, despite living with Sam, Newbie is following the same pattern.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:55.160
Can you imagine not going to the bathroom for four whole days?

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:55.880
So basically they can store a lot, for a long period of time.

00:42:55.720 --> 00:43:10.760
It's really important to know how their biology works because if we didn't know sloths are only supposed to poop and pee every four days, or every week, then we'd worry, like, "What's wrong with them?!

00:43:11.040 --> 00:43:16.840
So, now we're going to stick her at the base of the tree, this is her pooping tree.

00:43:16.080 --> 00:43:20.320
We have to be very quiet because she gets distracted easily.

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:28.560
No-one knows why sloths stick to the same latrine spot, but one theory is that they could be fertilising their favourite tree.

00:43:27.800 --> 00:43:31.320
(It's just a waiting game.

00:43:31.320 --> 00:43:36.120
(See how she's looking at me?

00:43:39.240 --> 00:43:42.240
(This could be good.

00:43:41.440 --> 00:43:46.080
But you have to look for the flick of the tail.

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:46.760
(If it goes like this, then she's going!

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:47.400
(But I'm not sure yet. )

00:43:52.160 --> 00:44:02.760
If you know that they really need to go, but they just don't feel comfortable enough to go, then it can be kind of stressful, cos you're like, "Please poop!"

00:44:01.880 --> 00:44:05.640
This is a bad sign.

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:08.520
She might not be quite ready.

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:09.240
I think we might have to try later, she's not ready.

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:12.280
I give up, you win.

00:44:17.640 --> 00:44:26.160
All Sam can do is bring Newbie back later for another try.

00:44:25.360 --> 00:44:31.760
It's a guessing game, and the ball is firmly in Newbie's court.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:36.720
Three hours later, and Sam and Newbie are ready to try again.

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:06.160
Her climbing right now is not exactly a good sign.

00:45:05.640 --> 00:45:09.680
She may still not be ready, even though she's like a beach ball.

00:45:10.240 --> 00:45:19.200
Newbie's stomach has four separate chambers, storing leafy food at different stages of digestion, a system unique to sloths.

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:26.120
And part of the reason why the whole process requires a great deal of patience.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:34.880
It's a bit like potty training a toddler - it's all in the timing.

00:45:36.080 --> 00:45:38.520
Two hours later and the heavens have opened.

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:41.120
Sam is hoping that Newbie's bowels will follow suit.

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:49.680
I'll try putting my hand on her a bit and see if it makes her feel a bit more secure.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:52.240
Oh, it's happening it's happening!

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:58.640
See the tail, that flip?

00:45:58.080 --> 00:45:59.720
I'm happy!

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:02.960
And Sam's not the only one.

00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:09.680
It must be such a relief to pee after four days.

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:18.120
I think I would close my eyes too!

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:33.840
Understandably, post-poo is the only time Sam is able to get an accurate measure of how much Newbie weighs.

00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:36.560
And establish how fit and healthy she is.

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:54.720
Nice!

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:57.160
1,000, even.

00:46:56.280 --> 00:47:02.160
So in two weeks she's gained about 75 grams, so that's really good.

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:05.160
I'm very happy that she's doing well today.

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:07.200
We have to take it one day at a time.

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:16.040
It's a promising sign, but there's a still a long way to go before Sam might feel confident enough to give Newbie a proper name.

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:30.400
In Southern Australia, at Cape Otway's Koala Rescue Centre, confidence is exactly what Danny needs.

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:36.560
It's his first day out in the enclosure full-time.

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:38.720
This is a huge milestone.

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:49.360
From now on there will be no more teddy and no more nights in the comfort and security of the indoors.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:53.040
Would you like to go up there, would that be fun?

00:47:52.440 --> 00:48:03.800
At six months old, a wild koala would be riding around with his mum outside of her pouch full-time, so Danny should be physically ready for his new challenge.

00:48:04.080 --> 00:48:10.240
He's climbing really beautifully, he's really agile and strong and really comfortable about navigating his way through the branches.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:11.440
He's really good.

00:48:11.320 --> 00:48:14.160
I'm very pleased with that.

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:21.000
But living without his human carers is about more than just climbing.

00:48:20.240 --> 00:48:26.360
Danny's buddies in the enclosure are Puddles and Tilly.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:31.040
These two rescued females have been here a year and are the equivalent of young teenagers.

00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:43.520
If one of them takes a shine to Danny, then he'll have a companion to make him feel safe and secure.

00:48:42.920 --> 00:48:47.280
Within minutes, Danny starts trying to get close to Tilly.

00:48:50.440 --> 00:48:58.480
Just like Danny, she was found on the side of the road underweight and dehydrated.

00:48:57.720 --> 00:49:04.880
But adult koalas are largely solitary in the wild and Tilly is not interested.

00:49:05.360 --> 00:49:18.400
But Danny isn't giving up just yet.

00:49:17.440 --> 00:49:22.080
He goes in to get some attention.

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:40.120
But Tilly makes her feelings clear.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:47.200
Danny decides to try his luck with Puddles.

00:49:49.920 --> 00:50:02.600
She has been through a lot in her young life, from being found without her mother by the road, to battling an infection that meant she lost most of her ears and several claws.

00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:10.840
Now fit and well, she seems to have a more laid-back approach to life.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:15.720
The early signs are promising.

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:21.760
But Puddles is still so young herself, Danny will have to hope that he can quietly win her over.

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:39.880
Over at Stella's place, Neil the wallaby needs a companion just as badly.

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:46.800
Since his unsuccessful introduction to the Three Amigos he's been on his own.

00:50:46.440 --> 00:50:50.080
But word has got out.

00:50:50.360 --> 00:50:57.720
There's another very young wallaby who is in as much need of a companion as Neil.

00:50:57.680 --> 00:51:00.720
Hello, Stella, I've got a little friend for you here.

00:51:00.080 --> 00:51:02.800
Is it a little boy or girl?

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:05.960
A little boy, his mum was hit by a car.

00:51:05.000 --> 00:51:07.360
Aw, he's gorgeous, what do you call him?

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:11.760
I think Jasper, I think that's what the person who found him would have named...

00:51:10.840 --> 00:51:12.520
Jasper, it is.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:13.720
Hi, Jasper.

00:51:12.920 --> 00:51:15.600
Welcome!

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:18.560
Thanks very much.

00:51:22.720 --> 00:51:23.880
All right.

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:30.720
Jasper has just come in and we have to weigh him to check on the weight, to compare him with Neil to see if he is a good size.

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:39.080
Jasper has to be just the right weight for the match to work.

00:51:38.200 --> 00:51:44.400
Neil weighs a kilo, so Jasper has to weigh a kilo, too.

00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:48.520
And he's one kilo, he's the perfect weight for Neil.

00:51:47.600 --> 00:52:00.200
When they hop into the same pouch they won't smother each other, and the chance of survival is much, much greater for both of them, so that's perfect.

00:51:59.320 --> 00:52:03.760
He's a little bit anxious because everything's new to him.

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:09.000
He's actually doing very well for such a brand-new little baby.

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:25.360
Look at that!

00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:26.120
There's somebody here to meet you.

00:52:48.560 --> 00:52:58.880
The gentleness of the two little creatures that have never met before is quite special.

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:12.640
Oh, there you go.

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:17.000
It's not long before the pair are confirming their bond.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:25.480
Very sweet.

00:53:27.720 --> 00:53:35.680
Watching them, when you see things like that, it's why you do what you do, why you become a wildlife carer.

00:53:36.760 --> 00:53:47.480
Having spent a bit of time with Stella, it has genuinely surprised me the amount of work that goes into raising these animals.

00:53:47.080 --> 00:53:56.000
It really gives you a sense of just how incredible the mothering in the wild really is.

00:53:55.520 --> 00:54:06.680
'And it also shows how incredible the urge is to find a substitute 'to fill the void left when they lose that crucial relationship.

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:19.600
'For Danny the koala, persuading Puddles to give him that 'close contact was critical to making his new life outside successful.'

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:31.080
Luckily his persistence has paid off, and she's taken him under her wing.

00:54:32.320 --> 00:54:40.080
The two of them snuggle up together day and night, and she even lets him ride around on her back, just like his real mother would.

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:45.040
It's a great result for Danny.

00:54:44.560 --> 00:54:54.240
Having this close contact with another koala will give his chances of making it back to the wild a huge boost.

00:54:54.480 --> 00:55:02.560
Both he and wallaby, Neil, have benefitted from dedicated and experienced carers and the company of their own species.

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:12.080
'Unfortunately it's a very different story in Costa Rica.

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:19.520
'Newbie's pneumonia has returned with a vengeance.'

00:55:19.960 --> 00:55:23.680
Hey, Sam, how's she doing?

00:55:27.040 --> 00:55:28.280
She sounds bad and she doesn't feel good.

00:55:27.400 --> 00:55:31.360
She struggles to breathe.

00:55:30.840 --> 00:55:37.600
I can see her chest move in and out.

00:55:46.720 --> 00:55:50.240
What's next for Newbie, how do you get to the bottom of this problem?

00:55:49.840 --> 00:56:01.360
We've started her on a different medication for the second round of the disease, and if that doesn't work, it's pretty serious.

00:56:00.560 --> 00:56:13.480
That's why I'm so concerned, because even though she's still hanging in there, if we can't cure her she'll never make it back to the wild, she probably won't even survive.

00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:14.080
It's tough.

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:14.680
I just want her to get better.

00:56:31.480 --> 00:56:47.520
I don't think I've ever seen Newbie looking quite so sorry for herself, and it's tough, because just as everyone was thinking she was getting better, Sam was even think about giving her a proper name for the first time...

00:56:47.560 --> 00:56:53.000
And now she's back on medication and the pneumonia is worse than ever.

00:56:52.400 --> 00:57:01.640
We've all got to just keep our fingers crossed that this new medication starts to work, and hopefully shakes off this pneumonia for good.

00:57:02.440 --> 00:57:09.280
'It's a waiting game once more for Sam and her orphaned sloth.

00:57:08.320 --> 00:57:14.040
'But she has made it this far, so there has to be hope that Newbie will pull through.'

00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:27.600
Next time, Al the ant eater has to be taught how to do what should come naturally.

00:57:26.800 --> 00:57:30.200
I'm afraid there's no termites in there, fella.

00:57:29.320 --> 00:57:34.840
Stacey, an orphaned wombat, hits a potentially devastating problem on her journey back to the wild.

00:57:34.000 --> 00:57:39.960
As you can hear in my voice, it is hard not to be attached to these guys.

00:57:39.040 --> 00:57:51.000
And Newbie continues her brave battle with pneumonia.

00:57:55.760 --> 00:58:03.080
The stage is set for the Party Conference Season 2014.

00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:15.800
Stay with BBC News for the key moments, including Conservative Party leader David Cameron's speech.

00:58:20.200 --> 00:58:21.280
On BBC TWO and with ongoing coverage on Radio 5 Live.

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:31.360
The Party Conferences 2014, as they happen.
